# HSDJ Equipment Marker Language

- Do not introduce generic numbered bullets, dot bullets, chevrons, or stock icons on public website surfaces when an approved HSDJ hardware marker can communicate the same hierarchy.
- Use the standard black EQ knob with its white indicator at twelve o'clock for list markers and repeated primary navigation markers.
- Keep the accompanying text semantic, visible, and understandable without recognizing the equipment reference.
- Hardware markers are decorative and must be hidden from assistive technology.
- A knob, pad, fader, meter, switch, or transport control must come from the shared HSDJ visual vocabulary and retain a consistent physical treatment across routes.
- Do not add DJ equipment merely to fill space. It must establish hierarchy, indicate state, support navigation, or explain a real relationship.
- Preserve visible focus, keyboard operation, touch targets, readable labels, mobile composition, and reduced-motion behavior.
- Reuse the system grammar across the site without stamping the same composition onto every page.
